A||||16||RZURCTN||84 SE 2.5L Help!!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||RZURCTN||12-07-1999||04:59 PM||||I finally got my engine in and started.  The problem is that as soon as it starts up it goes straight to 3000rpm.  I am receiving no trouble codes off the ECM.  If anyone can help me out here I would appreciate it!  Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-07-1999||05:26 PM||||Take your foot off the gas...duh.||||reg||12
Z||000002||RZURCTN||12-07-1999||05:32 PM||||I tried that too.  With my foot on the gas it was at 4000rpm, but with my foot off the gas it was still at 3000rpm!  I have everything hooked up right, even double checked it all.  All vacuum lines are going to the right places.  The car is in a nice, heated shop, so it's not like it's cold.  The temp guage even got up to 100 and it still idled high.  So your guess is going to have be be a little better Monkeyman! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||12-07-1999||05:53 PM||||I would have guessed an inoperative IAC valve, but I don't think the IAC is capable of generating more than 2500 rpm even when it's wide open...<P>Have you tried taking it for a spin?  Anytime I disconnected the battery, my 84 would idle at 2400 rpm until I took it for a drive at 50 mph or more, after which the idle came down to the normal 900 rpm. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-07-1999||05:56 PM||||Does it ever come down?  If so what does it settle to and how long does it take to get there.  It is common knowledge that the L-4 likes to rev high when first turned on.  Mine always... even in 100+ degrees Farenheit initially jumps up in excess of 2500 rpms.  Perhaps yours is relatively normal?  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000005||RZURCTN||12-07-1999||07:51 PM||||I haven't taken it for a drive yet.  Don't have the brake and rotors back on yet.  It should settle out after it has warmed up though.  It won't come down lower than 3000rpm.  Even if I blip the throttle a few times.  This doesn't seem to be right to me.  I don't think that it would be the IAC either, it worked fine before I took the engine out.  Also, adjusting the timing doesn't do anything for it either.  Any more guesses?||||reg||1
Z||000006||batboy||12-07-1999||08:44 PM||||This may be obvious and you already checked it, but is the accelerator cable binding or hanging up on something.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Cooter||12-07-1999||09:09 PM||||I thought the ECM did not reset the IAC valve until the car reached about 30 mph. If you have not driven the car, it will not know where the idle position of the IAC will be.||||reg||1
Z||000008||RZURCTN||12-07-1999||10:22 PM||||Batboy: No, the cable isn't binding or stuck.  The butterfly closes all the way.<P>Cooter:  If the car is up on jackstands and I get it up to 30mph will that work too?  Like if I engage the tranny and go through a few gears?  ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||12-07-1999||11:28 PM||||I dont like the idea of a car on a jack or stands running 30 mph myself, but if you are comfortable doing that- it is worth a try. I would have the engine at operating temp before I started spinning the wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Trixi||12-07-1999||11:29 PM||||CHecck the idler screw in the throttle body<BR>also you may try placing it on stands and getting it up to 30 <BR>it may go away<BR>probably where it has been liad up for a while.||||reg||10
Z||000011||2FAST4U||12-08-1999||02:14 AM||||Just a thought<P>Since it has been unused and has probably dried out real well. Is it possible that the injector in your TBI is sticking open?||||reg||1
Z||000012||Carrolles||12-08-1999||07:01 AM||||I had the same problem when I rebuilt my 2.8L.  I was sure I had checked all the vacuum lines but found the one from the PCV to the underside of the intake plenum disconnected at the fitting.  I reconnected it and the idle immediately went to 900 where it is supposed to be.<P>I'm not familiar with the vacuum routing on the 2.5 but take another look.  It sure sounds like a massive air leak somewhere.<P>Air leaking around the throttle body will cause high idle.  The TPS thinks it is doing its job but the O2 sensor tells it to pour in more gas to correct the O2 level in the exhaust.<P>Good luck,<BR>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000013||Rodrv6||12-08-1999||12:19 PM||||I had a similar problem on my 84.  It started idling VERY high for no apparent reason.  I had not had the computer or anything else disconnected.  I finally found the gasket for the egr valve had disintegrated and was leaking air past the valve flange into the intake manifold.  Hope this helps.||||reg||3
Z||000014||RZURCTN||12-08-1999||02:07 PM||||Thanks everyone, I'll give your suggestions a try and I'll let you know what I find, if anything.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Carrolles||12-09-1999||07:16 AM||||Keep in mind that if you do run the engine in gear while the car is on jack stands the wheel that is turning will turn double the speed indicated on the speedometer.  If you blip the throttle and show a 70 mph reading on the speedo the tire is actually turning at 140 mph and could come apart.||||reg||1
Z||000016||RZURCTN||12-09-1999||09:23 AM||||And the winner is........Carrolles!! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  It was an air leak past the TBI.  Got that corrected and it runs O.K.  Now I have a knock somewhere. And I'm getting a code 13.  And it's running really rich!  Time to change the O2 sensor?||||reg||1
